\date{2026-09-25}
\title{The homotopy #{n}-category}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\import{stt-macros}
\meta{source}{\PR{322}}
\meta{source}{\PR{314}}
\meta{source}{\PR{312}}
\tag{changelog}

\subtree{
\title{Summary}

\p{
I define [#{n}-truncated categories](stt-014A) and the
[homotopy #{n}-category of a type](stt-014F) using the newly added concept of
[relative separations](Modalities.GlobalRelativeSeparations) and
[relative suspensions](Core.RelativeSuspensions). Concretely, I define the
[directed suspension](stt-014N) #{\dirsuspension} as
#{\suspension_{\iota_{\partial\Delta^1}}}, which is the pushout
\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
\partial\Delta^1 \times A && \partial\Delta^1 \\
\\
\Delta^1 \times A && \dirsuspension A\nsp{.}
\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-3, phantom, "\ulcorner" very near end]
\end{tikzcd}
}

This construction comes with a functorial action on maps #{f\colon A \to B} which
completes the cube

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
\partial\Delta^1 \times A && \partial\Delta^1 \\
& \Delta^1 \times A && \dirsuspension A \\
\partial\Delta^1 \times B && \partial\Delta^1 \\
& \Delta^1 \times B && \dirsuspension B\nsp{.}
\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3, equals]
\arrow[from=2-2, to=2-4, crossing over]
\arrow[from=4-2, to=4-4, crossing over]
\arrow[from=2-2, to=4-2]
\arrow[from=2-4, to=4-4, "\dirsuspension f"]
\arrow[from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow[from=3-1, to=4-2]
\arrow[from=1-3, to=2-4]
\arrow[from=3-3, to=4-4]
\end{tikzcd}
}

This functorial action enables us to suspend the presentation of a localisation. From
this we obtain the [hom separation](stt-014C), and we record that
[localisation with respect to the directed suspension is the same as localising the
homs](stt-014H). The latter follows from the formal theory of relative separations.
In particular, we may [#{n}-truncate the homs](Synthetic.HomTruncation). An
#{n}-truncated category is then a category whose homs are #{n}-truncated. It follows
that [the underlying type of an #{n}-truncated category is #{n}-truncated](stt-014I).
The homotopy #{n}-category of a type is the universal reflection into #{n}-truncated
categories. Concretely, it is localisation at the family of maps
##{\{\Lambda^2_1 \hookrightarrow \Delta^2, \mathfrak{I} \to \b1, \dirsuspension\sphere{n} \to \Delta^1\} \cup \{(i \le j) \lor (j \le i) \to \b1 \mid i,j : \Delta^1 \}\nsp{,}}
where #{\mathfrak{I}} is the [walking isomorphism](stt-00P7) and #{\sphere{n}} is the
[#{n}-sphere](stt-002O).
}

\p{
The homotopy #{n}-category is useful for computing things like the colimit of a
diagram: if #{\mathcal{C}} is an #{n}-truncated category and #{F : I \to \mathcal{C}}
is any diagram in #{\mathcal{C}}, then the colimit of #{F} agrees with the colimit of
#{F\circ\eta : h_n(I) \to \mathcal{C}} where #{h_n(I)} is the homotopy #{n}-category
of #{I}. For #{n = 0} this recovers \citet{Observation 8.6.10}{Hau25}.
}
}

